=== layout: one-plus-two-panels

# [Biochemistry Lecture]{color=#6B638F}

@left

- [*Structure explains function*]{color=#6B638F}
  - We go into great detail on protein structure, enzyme function, and other macromolecules.
  - Protein purification, separation methods, and enzyme kinetics.
- [*Protein-structure assignments*]{color=#6B638F}
  - Make images in UCSF Chimera and explain what they show.
  - Connect a molecule's structure with what it does.

@top-right

![Upper-Level Introductory Biochemistry textbook cover](repo:evergreen/assets/upcoming_courses/biochemistry_textbook_cover.png)

@bottom-right

BCHM 355-20 / 455-20, [Spring 2027]{color=#6B638F}; 3 credits\
Thursdays, 2:00-4:30 p.m.; SCH 612\
January 19-May 10 (Session 1)\
CRNs: 355 = 24780; 455 = 24781

@citation

Textbook cover: Biology LibreTexts. https://bio.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/93423/mindtouch.page%2523thumbnail?revision=5

=== layout: one-panel

# [Biochemistry Lab]{color=#D40000}

@body

- BCHM 356-20 / 456-20, [Spring 2027]{color=#D40000}; 3 credits
  - Tuesdays, 10:00 a.m.-1:55 p.m.; SCH 550
  - Face-to-face, Schaumburg; January 19-May 10 (Session 1)
- [*Less structured and more independent study-like*]{color=#D40000}
  - Enzyme assays and kinetics: measure reaction rates and interpret the data.
  - Isolation and analysis of biological macromolecules.
  - Explore protein sequence, structure, and folding using molecular software and databases.
- [*Experimental design and analysis*]{color=#D40000}
  - Data collection, graphs, and interpretation are part of the lab work.

=== layout: one-panel

# [Cellular and Molecular Biology]{color=#24578F}

@body

- BIOL 301, [Spring 2027]{color=#24578F}
  - [My teaching assignment is not confirmed yet.]{color=#D40000}
- [*The chemistry of life*]{color=#24578F}
  - Biological molecules and how they work in cells.
  - Cell structure, membranes, transport, and communication.
  - Cellular energy: respiration and photosynthesis.
  - DNA, gene expression, and regulation.
- [*Lecture, laboratory, and discussion*]{color=#24578F}

=== layout: two-panels

# [Biology and Ethics in Film]{color=#45405C}

@left

- BIOL 383-20 / 483-20, [Summer 2027]{color=#45405C}; 3 credits
  - Tuesdays/Thursdays, 6-9 p.m., over Zoom
  - July 6-August 9 (Session D)
  - CRNs: BIOL 383 = 32156; BIOL 483 = 32157

- [*Example movie schedules*]{color=#45405C}
  - [Spring 2022](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1a2F5pshoAtGjknMw3oUXjmI4SEjpVIvc1xiNHdJsD6Q/edit)
  - [Summer 2024](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1L2NZcMY99Nfo7RsXeIcBDXjEkrtjD9shhZandkivfPc/edit)
  - [Summer 2026](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1O5rjL8j6IocYQdYKjUiwwuhyOEoRfpEuqCcYse3VF8c/edit)

@right

- [*Watch a feature film, then discuss*]{color=#45405C}
  - Explore its biology, science, and ethical questions.
  - Give quick post-film "hot takes" and make a group movie-review podcast.
- [*Weekly journal prompts*]{color=#45405C}
  - Make a case, include your opinions, and think critically.
  - Most of the grade is a weekly writing prompt; getting it done is key.
